# Packages `ciphr-run` as an image whose filesystem is that one binary and the
# notices its dependencies' licences require to travel with it.
#
# ── Why an image for a single file ───────────────────────────────────────────
# `ciphr-run` is bind-mounted into images this project does not own (ADR-14), so
# what a deployment needs is the file, not something to run. `release.yml`
# attaches it to the tag, and for a public repository that is the whole answer.
# While the repository is private it is not: a release asset is readable only by
# something that can authenticate to GitHub, and the host that has to mount the
# file authenticates to a registry and not to the forge. The registry is GHCR
# since 2026-08-24, when the second build into an internal one was removed --
# which makes this image the only channel that carries the wrapper to a host, and
# the release asset the channel for anything that can authenticate to GitHub.
#
# ── Why `scratch` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is nothing else this image should be able to do. It is a transport, not
# a runtime: no shell, no libc, no package manager, so a container built from it
# can execute nothing but the wrapper. Three text files under `/licenses` are the
# only other contents and change none of that -- they are the notices MIT, BSD
# and ISC require to accompany a copy of their code, and this image is the only
# channel that carries the wrapper to a host that cannot authenticate to GitHub.
# Leaving them out would have made this the one artefact whose licence conditions
# were unmet. That is also why the binary has to be
# statically linked — a dynamically linked one would work here and fail in the
# foreign image it is mounted into, which is the deployment that finds out.
# `ci/build-wrapper.sh` verifies the linkage rather than trusting the target
# name, and it is the same script CI runs, so this image cannot be built from a
# binary that would not have passed.
#
# ── How the file gets out ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#     docker create --name ciphr-run-export <image>@<digest>
#     docker cp ciphr-run-export:/ciphr-run ./ciphr-run
#     docker rm ciphr-run-export
#
# The container is never started; `docker cp` reads the filesystem of a created
# one, which is why an image with no shell is no obstacle. `release.yml` runs
# exactly these three steps against the image it has just pushed and computes
# the published checksum from what comes out, so the retrieval path a deployment
# follows is the one that produced the number it checks against.

# ── Stage 1: build ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The same digest-pinned base as `Dockerfile`, for the reason given there: a tag
# is a name that can be moved. Deliberately the same one, so the two images
# cannot drift apart on the compiler that built them.
FROM rust:1.94-bookworm@sha256:6ae102bdbf528294bc79ad6e1fae682f6f7c2a6e6621506ba959f9685b308a55 AS builder

WORKDIR /build

# The musl target needs its own linker, and `rustup target add` does not supply
# one. Without this the build fails at link time with a message about `cc`,
# which is a long way from "the target toolchain is incomplete".
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      musl-tools && \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

COPY Cargo.toml Cargo.lock rust-toolchain.toml ./
COPY crates/ crates/
COPY ci/build-wrapper.sh ci/

# The script, not a bare `cargo build`: the static-linkage check and the size
# budget are the two properties that make this file safe to mount into somebody
# else's container, and duplicating them here would let them drift from the ones
# CI enforces.
#
# It names the binary after its target triple, and the two lines after the build
# undo that here rather than naming the triple in the `COPY` below. The triple is
# then defined in `ci/build-wrapper.sh` and nowhere else, which is the whole point:
# `1f82928` renamed the asset in that script and in `release.yml` and left this
# file copying `/out/ciphr-run`, so `v0.6.0` published its server image and failed
# to build its wrapper. The path *inside* the image is fixed by ADR-14 and by every
# document that says `docker cp …:/ciphr-run`, so it must not follow the asset name.
#
# The checksum is removed rather than kept: it would otherwise match the glob, and
# the checksum of a file sitting beside the file is worth nothing anyway. A glob
# that ever matches two binaries makes `mv` fail loudly, which is the right
# direction for the day a second architecture appears.
# `TARGETARCH` is set by buildx for the platform being built, and the target has
# to follow it. Without this the script took its default and an arm64 builder
# tried to cross-compile x86_64 with the native `musl-gcc`, which fails inside
# `ring` with `unrecognized command-line option '-m64'` -- a message that says
# nothing about the actual mistake. Caught by the `Images (arm64)` job in
# `ci.yml` on the pull request that added it, which is where a thing like this
# is supposed to be caught rather than during a release.
#
# An architecture with no musl target here is refused rather than defaulted: the
# alternative is building the wrong binary and finding out when somebody mounts
# it.
ARG TARGETARCH
RUN set -eu; \
    case "$TARGETARCH" in \
      amd64) target=x86_64-unknown-linux-musl ;; \
      arm64) target=aarch64-unknown-linux-musl ;; \
      *) echo "no musl target known for TARGETARCH=$TARGETARCH" >&2; exit 1 ;; \
    esac; \
    TARGET="$target" sh ci/build-wrapper.sh /out; \
    rm -f /out/*.sha256; \
    mv /out/ciphr-run-* /out/ciphr-run

# ── Stage 2: the artefact ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FROM scratch

COPY --from=builder /out/ciphr-run /ciphr-run

# Under `/licenses` rather than beside the binary at `/`, so that the documented
# retrieval path -- `docker cp <container>:/ciphr-run` -- still names one file and
# nothing has to change in `release.yml` or in `docs/operations/wrapper.md`.
COPY LICENSE-MIT LICENSE-APACHE THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md /licenses/

# An entrypoint for two reasons, neither of them "so this can be a service".
# `docker create` refuses an image that names no command, and that is the
# supported way to get the file out. And `docker run --rm <image> --help` then
# prints the wrapper's own usage, so an image containing one undocumented binary
# can still say what it is.
ENTRYPOINT ["/ciphr-run"]
